{"key": "3168a5a3d8e4", "skip": false, "context": "A research team studying how people react to AI decision-makers found that people's satisfaction tracked perceived fairness of the process. A commenter pointed out that a large existing social-science literature on procedural fairness already documents this pattern for human decision-makers, and suggested replicating those studies with an AI as the decision-maker. The team agreed the existing literature was large and relevant. They then had to decide what follow-up work to do at the intersection of that literature and AI.", "question": "What empirical studies should the team pursue at the intersection of AI decision-making and the existing procedural-fairness literature?"},
{"key": "f95ab32f9e4f", "skip": false, "context": "A researcher who had given AI-safety talks to over a hundred academics described lessons learned about which framings were persuasive. He had noted that leading with existential-risk motivation did not seem convincing, and his poster materials still referred to catastrophic outcomes from more-powerful-than-human AI. A commenter asked whether that itself counted as alarmism. He had to decide how to characterize the role and strength of existential-risk claims in his messaging.", "question": "How should the researcher describe the place of existential-risk claims when advising others on talking persuasively to academics about AI risk?"},
{"key": "539b672975f0", "skip": false, "context": "A researcher reported that, in his experience talking to over a hundred academics, post-docs and professors were the most dismissive of AI safety, though with high variance. A commenter asked which subset of senior academics did take him seriously and whether he had data on them or his talks, arguing that identifying their characteristics could have high returns. He had to decide whether his informal observations could support a useful general claim about which senior academics are receptive.", "question": "Can the researcher draw a reliable inference from his small, informal sample about which kinds of senior academics take AI safety seriously?"},
{"key": "fcb075820b79", "skip": false, "context": "A post argued that 'foresight' is a distinct and useful practice for AGI safety strategy, different from 'forecasting', giving two reasons: forecasting is often understood as time-bounded prediction, and foresight considers a cone of possible outcomes rather than a single prediction. A commenter was skeptical, believing forecasting is not time-dependent and already aims at a probability-weighted distribution over outcomes, which he considered strictly better than an unweighted cone. The team behind the post had to decide whether to maintain that foresight is a meaningfully separate field or treat it as part of forecasting and strategy work.", "question": "Should the team treat foresight as a distinct field from forecasting for AGI safety strategy, and on what basis?"},
{"key": "78525ac85420", "skip": false, "context": "A researcher had written about disagreements regarding whether recursive self-improvement could produce rapid intelligence gains. A commenter argued that dismissal of recursive self-improvement was an outdated cached thought, pointing to fast progress in coding and mathematics capabilities, the feeding of scientific corpora into large language models, and heavy corporate investment in coding models, and suggested a criticality threshold might be near. The researcher replied that he was unsure whether the comment supported or opposed his disagreements. He had to decide how to interpret this ambiguous evidence and argument.", "question": "How should the researcher interpret the recent capability trends and investment patterns as evidence about whether recursive self-improvement is near?"},
{"key": "2833b4b2f1f5", "skip": false, "context": "A research team had published a model predicting future GPU performance in FLOP/s. A commenter suggested that, if a performance limit is reached, the next frontier would be reducing the price of compute, and asked about current bottlenecks on cost reduction—effectively proposing a follow-up on FLOP per dollar rather than FLOP/s alone. The team had to decide whether extending their analysis to price-performance was worth pursuing.", "question": "Should the team extend their GPU performance model to forecast compute per dollar rather than raw compute per second?"},
{"key": "59090d397da1", "skip": false, "context": "A research team modeling future GPU performance listed several physical limitations in their analysis. A commenter noted that the Landauer limit—a fundamental thermodynamic bound on energy per operation, which current devices approach within roughly three orders of magnitude—was missing and might be the most relevant limit. The team had in fact considered modeling it explicitly. They had to decide whether to include it as an explicit term in the model.", "question": "Should the team include the Landauer limit as an explicit term in their GPU performance model?"},
{"key": "fb67f995644c", "skip": false, "context": "A research team had produced a forecast of GPU compute improvements. A commenter suggested feeding it straightforwardly into an existing biological-anchors timelines model to see the implications. The team looked into this partly to better understand the model's assumption that price-performance doubles every 2.5 years—an estimate some had called too conservative, though the team's own analysis suggested the long-run doubling time could be slower, while specialization or breakthroughs might justify 2 years or less. They had to decide whether and how to incorporate their compute forecast into the timelines model given this uncertainty.", "question": "Should the team incorporate their GPU compute forecast into the biological-anchors timelines model, and if so how should they handle uncertainty about the price-performance doubling time?"}
]}
